EXPERIMENT 3
NETWORK PROTOCOL ANALYSIS USING WIRESHARK
Date: 31/12/2025
AIM
To familiarise the working of packet sniffing and analysis tool – Wireshark.
THEORY
Wireshark is a highly versatile and widely used open-source network packet analyzer. It lets users
capture, inspect, and analyze network traffic in real time or from saved files. Wireshark is a powerful
tool for network troubleshooting, monitoring, and security analysis. It provides detailed information
about individual network packets, including their source, destination, protocol, payload, and timing,
making it an invaluable resource for diagnosing network issues, identifying anomalies, and enhancing
network security. Wireshark is available for multiple platforms, including Linux, Windows, and
macOS, and is commonly employed by network administrators, security professionals, and developers
to gain insights into network behavior and resolve connectivity and security challenges.
Wireshark can be used in the following ways:
• It is used by network security engineers to examine security problems.
• It allows the users to watch all the traffic being passed over the network.
• It is used by network engineers to troubleshoot network issues.
• It also helps to troubleshoot latency issues and malicious activities on your network.
• It can also analyze dropped packets.
• It helps us to know how all the devices like laptop, mobile phones, desktop, switch, routers,
etc., communicate in a local network or the rest of the world. Features of Wireshark:
• Multi-platform software: Wireshark runs on multiple operating systems, including Linux,
Windows, and macOS.
• Three-pane packet browser: It uses a standard interface with three panes to present packet
details clearly.
• Deep protocol inspection: Wireshark can deeply inspect hundreds of network protocols.
• Live network analysis: It allows real-time data capture from various network types, such as
Ethernet and loopback
• Sorting and filtering options: Users can easily filter and sort captured data for focused
analysis.
• VoIP analysis: Wireshark includes specialized tools for analyzing VoIP traffic.
• USB traffic capture: It can capture raw USB traffic, providing broader analysis beyond
traditional networks.
• Customizable settings: Filters, timers, and other settings allow precise control over what data
is captured and displayed.
• File format support: It supports well-documented capture file formats such as PcapNg and
Libpcap, which are used to store captured data.
• Top software for network analysis: Wireshark is highly regarded and widely used for tasks
such as tracing unauthorized traffic, analysing firewall settings, and troubleshooting network
issues
ALGORITHM
1. Double-click on wireshark to open it.
2. We will get the traffic traversing through the current interface.
3. Select the wi-fi option
4. Click Capture from the menu bar and click start. Then it shows the traffic with the packet
content written in hexadecimal or ASCII format. And the information above the packet
content, is the details of the packet header. It will continue listening to all the data packets,
and you will get a lot of data. If you want to see a particular data, then you can click on the
red button. The traffic will be stationary, and you can note the parameters like time, source,
destination, the protocol being used, length, and the Info. To view in-depth details, you can
click on that particular address; a lot of the information will be displayed below that. There
will be detailed information on HTTP packets, TCP packets, etc. The red button is shown
below:
The screen/interface of the Wireshark is divided into five parts:
● First part contains a menu bar and the options displayed below it. This part is at
the top of the window. File and the capture menus options are commonly used
in Wireshark. The capture menu allows to start the capturing process. And the
File menu is used to open and save a capture file.
● The second part is the packet listing window. It determines the packet flow or the
captured packets in the traffic. It includes the packet number, time, source,
destination, protocol, length, and info. We can sort the packet list by clicking on
the column name.
● Next comes the packet header- detailed window. It contains detailed information
about the components of the packets. The protocol info can also be expanded or
minimized according to the information required.
● The bottom window called the packet contents window, which displays the
content in ASCII and hexadecimal format.
● At last, is the filter field which is at the top of the display. The captured packets
on the screen can be filtered based on any component according to your
requirements. For example, if we want to see only the packets with the HTTP
protocol, we can apply filters to that option. All the packets with HTTP as the
protocol will only be displayed on the screen.
5. There is a filter block below the menu bar, from where a large amount of data can be
filtered. For example, if we apply a filter for HTTP, only the interfaces with the HTTP will
be listed. We can apply filters for TCP,UDP ,ARP,ICMP
6. Steps for the permanent colorization are: click on the ‘View’ option on the menu bar
and select ‘Coloring Rules’. Select the option ‘View’ and then choose ‘Colorize Packet
List’ which is used to toggle the color on and off. Whenever we type any commands in the
filter command box, it turns green if your command is correct. It turns red if it is incorrect
or the Wireshark does not recognize your command
